# The Tale of the River Sisters

In ancient times, two sister spirits ruled a divided kingdom. Dawn lived upstream where the river sprang cold and pure from the mountains. Dusk dwelt downstream where the waters met the sea.

Each season, Dawn could choose how much water to release downstream. Whatever she sent would gather strength, multiplying threefold as it flowed through the enchanted valley. Dusk would then decide how much of this abundance to send back upstream as mist and rain.

For many seasons, they trusted perfectly. Dawn sent generously; Dusk returned fairly. The kingdom flourished.

But one autumn, Dawn grew suspicious. She heard whispers that Dusk kept too much water for the coastal plains. In fear, Dawn held back the river to a trickle. The threefold magic weakened. Dusk, receiving little, could return even less.

Dawn saw the meager mist and felt vindicated. Dusk saw the trickle and felt betrayed. Each thought the other had broken faith first.

The kingdom withered through winter.

Only when a wise crane traveled between them, carrying the truth, did they understand: their fear had created the very betrayal they dreaded.

Trust, once broken by suspicion, requires more courage to rebuild than to maintain.
